importanttext = """The perception of facial typicality refers to the degree to which a face is considered a representative example of a category, such as a racial group or the general human population [1]. The concept of a “typical” face is inherently population-specific, with typicality defined relative to the average appearance of that group. This perception is influenced by physical facial attributes, such as shape, symmetry, and other morphological characteristics, and is closely associated with social judgments like trustworthiness [2]. Experimental studies have consistently shown that distinctive faces are recognized more easily and recalled more accurately than typical faces [3,4,5,6,7]. Such distinctiveness is usually due to an unusual feature or combination of features (e.g., a large nose or an elongated face), or it can be enhanced using computer graphics that caricature the face (e.g., [8]). Research further indicates that more typical faces are often perceived as more trustworthy, while perceived attractiveness can increase beyond the typical range, suggesting that typicality and attractiveness represent separate dimensions of facial evaluation [9,10].
The perception of facial typicality is commonly conceptualized within a cognitive “face space” model, in which faces are encoded as vectors and evaluated relative to a prototypical or average face, and this process is influenced by familiarity and in-group bias [11]. A useful framework has been proposed by Tim Valentine’s norm-based multidimensional face-space model (navnMDFS), in which each face is represented as a point in a multidimensional space defined by dimensions relevant for discriminating between faces. Individual faces are positioned according to their distance from a central norm—an average derived from all faces an individual has encountered [12,13]. Within this “face space”, averaging many faces of similar typicality creates a prototypical representation that serves as a cognitive reference point. Distinctiveness can therefore be understood as the distance of a face from this average. The face norm acts as the origin of the multidimensional space within which faces are encoded, stored, compared, and recognized (e.g., [12,13,14,15]). Faces located far from the average (and thus further apart from other faces) are perceived as distinctive, while those situated near the densely populated center of the space, and close to the average, are perceived as typical. Consequently, distinctive faces are more accurately and confidently recognized in a recognition task because there are fewer opportunities for them to be confused with nearby neighbors.
Exemplar-based models offer a complementary perspective, proposing that faces are encoded relative to other experienced faces rather than a single average representation [16]. Notably, face space models that implement either a norm-referenced or an exemplar-based encoding of faces can account for phenomena like face distinctiveness and caricature effects.
The assessment of face typicality and distinctiveness builds on these theoretical frameworks. Typically, it involves creating an average “composite” face for a population and evaluating how individual faces deviate from this average. Face typicality reflects how closely a face resembles the population average, while distinctiveness represents a unique deviation from the norm [1]. These qualities can be measured using both subjective ratings [17] and computational methods [18], and they have been shown to influence how people perceive faces, affecting judgments of attractiveness, trustworthiness, and how well faces are remembered [19].
Computational methods involve generating an average face by morphing multiple faces from a population, providing a visual benchmark for typicality [20]. Individual faces can then be compared to this composite using distance measures such as Procrustes Distance—which quantifies shape differences after alignment (e.g., [21,22,23])—and Euclidean Distance—which directly measures the distance between a face’s features and the population average within a “face space” (e.g., [24]).
People can also reliably rate facial typicality and distinctiveness using Likert scales, with research consistently showing high inter-rater agreement for these ratings (e.g., [25]). Such findings indicate that subjective ratings provide a stable and meaningful measure of facial characteristics [26] and suggest that humans share a common understanding of facial prototypes and deviations from them [27], which is used in tasks like face recognition and the formation of social impressions [2].
Building on this, the present study provides facial typicality–distinctiveness norms for a set of 304 faces from the Glasgow Unfamiliar Face Database (GUFD) using Likert scales. The GUFD includes multiple images of each identity, offering a rich resource for experimental studies of face perception, memory, and social judgment. Importantly, facial typicality and distinctiveness are core dimensions in face perception and recognition research, as they systematically influence encoding, recognition accuracy, memory performance, and social judgments such as perceived trustworthiness. Classifying facial stimuli along these dimensions, therefore, allows researchers to control factors that directly affect cognitive and perceptual outcomes. By providing subjective norms for the front-facing images of identities from the GUFD, the present study addresses a gap in the literature and supports researchers in selecting stimuli with greater control over key facial dimensions in a widely used stimulus set. These norms contribute to a better understanding of cognitive mechanisms underlying face recognition. They may also support studies of social perception and face memory, allow more precise analyses in forensic contexts (e.g., evaluating police suspect lineups), and facilitate the development and evaluation of computational vision algorithms."""
